{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>I would scatter them</b>. The grammarians said: אֲפַזְּרֵם בְּכָל פֵּאָה (I would scatter them in every direction). If so, the meaning of <b>I said</b> would be that there was a will before Him in the attribute of judgment that they should thus forever be scattered in every direction, and that their memory would cease from mankind forever, were it not that the anger of the enemy was gathered. In my opinion, it is a compound word, as they mentioned in Sifrei (Ha'azinu 326): אַף אֵי הֵם — but its meaning is that I would make them cease (אֲשִׁיתֵם) in the manner of פֵּאָה and in the sense that people would say of them אֵי הֵם (where are they), meaning that no name or remnant would remain for them among the nations, and their place would not be known where it is. Now this hints at the exile of the ten tribes who were exiled to the river Gozan (II Kings 17:6), which the Sages call Sambatyon, for Gozan is called so from the language of וַיָּגָז שַׂלְוִים (and the quails were driven) (Numbers 11:31), אַתָּה גוֹזִי (You shall drive) (Psalms 71:6), for those who stand behind it are hidden from the children of men. And they call it Sambatyon because of its resting on the Sabbath, for the day of the Sabbath in that language is Sabt, as it is in Arabic, and it is customary in their language to add 'yon' to descriptives: ezovyon, choryon, mulyon, and so many others. [n: Ramban's etymology connects גוזן/Gozan with גז\"ה (to drive/cut), and explains סמבטיון/Sambatyon as derived from שבת/Sabbath + the suffix -יון.]" "<b>I would make their memory cease from mankind</b>. Our exile among the peoples — we of Judah and Benjamin — is such that we have no remembrance among the peoples, nor are we reckoned as a people or nation at all. Now Scripture says that according to the attribute of judgment it would have been so, that we should remain in exile forever, were it not for the anger of the enemy. And this teaches that in our exile now the merit of the fathers is exhausted, and we have no deliverance from the hand of the peoples except for the sake of His great Name, may He be blessed, as the meaning of what He said in Ezekiel (20:41–44): And I will gather you from the lands where you were scattered in them, and I will be sanctified in you before the eyes of the nations, and you shall know that I am YHWH, when I act with you for My Name's sake, not according to your evil ways and according to your corrupt deeds, O house of Israel — and so it is further stated (ibid. v. 9): And I acted for My Name's sake, so as not to profane it before the eyes of the nations, etc. Therefore Moses mentioned in his prayer (Numbers 14:15): And the nations who heard Your Name will say, etc., and HaShem, blessed be He, acknowledged him in this (ibid. v. 20): And YHWH said, I have forgiven according to your word. And the reason for this argument is not to demonstrate His power among His enemies, for all the nations are as nothing before Him, reckoned to Him as emptiness and void — but HaShem created man among the lower beings so that he might recognize his Creator and give thanks to His Name, and He placed free will in his hand to do evil or to do good. And when they all sinned willingly and denied Him, none remained except this people for His Name, and He made known among them through signs and wonders that He is Elohim of gods and Lord of lords, and through this it became known to all the peoples. Now if He were to turn and destroy their memory, the peoples would forget His signs and His deeds, and they would no longer be recounted; and if a man were to mention them, they would think it was a force among the forces of the constellations and the stars, and that it had passed and vanished. And so the purpose of the creation of man would be utterly nullified, so that none who knows his Creator would remain among them — only those who provoke Him. Therefore it is fitting according to the attribute of will that was at the creation of the world, that there should be a will before Him to maintain for Himself a people for all days, for they are the ones near to Him and the ones who know Him among all the peoples. And this is the meaning of For YHWH will judge His people and relent concerning His servants (Deuteronomy 32:36): that HaShem will remember in mercy that they are His people from of old, and He will remember that they are His servants who stood for Him in their exile like servants, enduring the afflictions and the subjugation — as the meaning of what is stated (Isaiah 63:8): And He said, Surely they are My people, children who will not deal falsely. And I have already hinted, regarding the creation of man, at a sublime and hidden sod, which requires that we become a people to Him and that He be for us an Elohim, as the meaning of what is stated (Isaiah 43:7): Everyone who is called by My Name, for My glory I created him, etc. [n: ית' is resolved as יתברך, 'may He be blessed,' following standard Rabbinic abbreviation.]"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :register {:flags {1 ["Lord"]}}, :notes {0 "Ramban derives Gozan from roots meaning 'to drive away' (גזה/גוז), explaining that the Sambatyon river hides the exiled tribes; Sambatyon is explained as Sabbath (sabt) + the Romance/Greek diminutive suffix -yon.", 1 "Resolved ית' as יתברך (may He be blessed) based on context; the abbreviated form is unusual and could also theoretically expand to יתהלל, but the conventional reading fits the reverent register here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:58:52.941849669Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}